ADDITIONAL PERMISSION -- NVIDIA CUDA
------------------------------------

  Additional permission under GNU GPL version 3 section 7

  If you modify this Program, or any covered work, by linking or combining it
  with the NVIDIA CUDA Toolkit, the NVIDIA CUDA runtime and driver libraries,
  the NVIDIA CUDA Core Compute Libraries (CCCL, comprising Thrust, CUB and
  libcu++), and/or the NVIDIA math libraries distributed with the CUDA Toolkit
  (including but not limited to cuBLAS, cuSOLVER, cuSPARSE, cuRAND and cuFFT),
  or with modified versions of those libraries, the licensors of this Program
  grant you additional permission to convey the resulting work.

  Corresponding Source for a non-source form of such a combination shall
  include the source code for the parts of the NVIDIA libraries used as well as
  that of the covered work.


NOTES (NON-NORMATIVE)
---------------------

The following notes explain the scope and intent of the permission above. They
are explanatory only and grant nothing in themselves.

1. WHY THIS EXISTS. DiFfRG can optionally be built with a CUDA backend
   (-DUSE_CUDA=ON; OFF by default). Such a build links against the proprietary
   NVIDIA CUDA runtime. The GPL's "System Libraries" exception (GPLv3 section 1)
   is generally understood to cover the CUDA runtime, since it ships as part of
   the normal packaging of the CUDA Toolkit, which also provides the nvcc
   compiler used to produce the work. That argument is widely relied upon by
   other GPL-licensed scientific codes with CUDA backends, but it has never been
   tested in court. This permission removes the need to rely on it as far as
   DiFfRG's own code is concerned.

2. WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER. The DiFfRG copyright holders can only grant
   additional permissions for code they hold copyright in. DiFfRG also links
   third-party GPL and LGPL libraries whose copyright is held by others --
   most importantly GSL (GPL-3.0-or-later), and deal.II (LGPL-2.1-or-later).
   This permission does not, and cannot, extend to those. Anyone distributing a
   CUDA-enabled *binary* of DiFfRG therefore still relies on the System
   Libraries exception with respect to GSL. Note that this exception is part of
   the GPL itself rather than something a licensor grants, so it applies to GSL
   on the same terms as to DiFfRG.

   This does not affect distribution of DiFfRG in source form, which is how it
   is published: GPL obligations attach to conveying a work, and building
   privately for one's own use is not conveying.

3. CCCL AND THE NVIDIA SOFTWARE LICENSE. CCCL is predominantly licensed under
   Apache-2.0 with LLVM Exception, with CUB under BSD-3-Clause and portions of
   Thrust under BSL-1.0 and a FreeBSD licence -- all compatible with the GPL. A
   small number of libcu++ headers are instead covered by a proprietary NVIDIA
   Software License, whose terms are not GPL-compatible. In CCCL v2.7.0 (the
   version DiFfRG pins) these are exactly four files:

       libcudacxx/include/cuda/pipeline
       libcudacxx/include/cuda/annotated_ptr
       libcudacxx/include/cuda/std/detail/__annotated_ptr
       libcudacxx/include/cuda/std/detail/__access_property

   DiFfRG does not use any of them, and none is reachable from the CCCL headers
   it does include. See the CCCL entry in third_party.txt for the verification.
   The permission above nonetheless names CCCL, so that the position does not
   depend on that continuing to hold.
